00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:07.070
Glyn Povah: insights about customers. Has there been a SIM swap? Have they ported to another network?

137
00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:17.239
Glyn Povah: Has the number been recycled? You know, all of these events are critical for reinforcing and underpinning the network identity when you're going to build these kind of solutions. And the good news is.

138
00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:24.360
Glyn Povah: Yeah, we're fairly advanced now at building these APIs, real-time APIs, to surface this information, both internally and externally.

139
00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:36.250
Glyn Povah: But also, I think we saw it as an opportunity to… to reach out into the wider ecosystem and look at other capabilities, and then we came across Doc Labs and Nick and the team.

140
00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:41.549
Glyn Povah: And look to put together a solution with a number of different technologies.

141
00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:50.519
Glyn Povah: Where we thought, actually, it made sense to really create a robust, Sorry, service and ecosystem where

142
00:15:50.960 --> 00:16:04.809
Glyn Povah: where we could actually involve consumers in the loop as well, and put privacy, you know, on a stronger footing. And I guess, you know, going back to 1992 and the years that followed, you know, we did have a trusted ecosystem because

143
00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:11.159
Glyn Povah: In those days, we would have, you know, O2 and Vodafone and maybe T-Mobile when they came along. I was thinking about the UK.

144
00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:21.169
Glyn Povah: But, you know, most markets had 2 or 3 domestic carriers that eventually got connected to each other, and when the phone numbers were passed from one network to another, they could be trusted.

145
00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:27.229
Glyn Povah: And the reason for that was there was no global interconnect as such. You know, what has happened…

146
00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:39.859
Glyn Povah: since those days, and especially in the UK, driven by our regulators, our regulators insisted on having more and more competition, which means that, you know, we have to connect partners when they make a request.

147
00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:56.450
Glyn Povah: And it's a reasonable request, and that has, you know, generated the benefit of huge amounts of competition, lower prices for consumers, and all those good things that we all want, but it's also created, you know, very, very significant numbers of interconnects on a global basis from network to network.

148
00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:09.389
Glyn Povah: making it very hard for carriers to sometimes understand the origin. They obviously know the destination if they're the terminating carrier, but the origin of those calls or messages sometimes can be quite hard to trace.

149
00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:28.699
Glyn Povah: So they… so that has taken us into a situation where, you know, scammers are exploiting, you know, gaps and loopholes in this global interconnected carrier network. And the power of that network, of course, is you can call anyone or message anyone anywhere on the globe, simply by knowing their phone number. The downside of that is…

150
00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.030
Glyn Povah: With the advent of all this interconnect, and…

151
00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:48.559
Glyn Povah: and all the other API-based services, scammers have managed to find ways into that, and they're exploiting that. And therefore, trust in our networks is declining, and so we're looking for new solutions under the GSMA,

152
00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:58.619
Glyn Povah: United Against Scams banner and initiative to really bring back trust to the carrier networks, for our core services, voice and messaging, and obviously our subscribers are a key part of that.

153
00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:07.139
Glyn Povah: So this… so this particular POC is all about call center authentication, so it's all about…

154
00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:24.660
Glyn Povah: allowing the call center to authenticate you or me as we call into the call center, but I'm moving away from traditional technologies. Now, traditional technologies, as I mentioned, for doing this, will be using CLI, which is having the phone number passed by the carrier network, through the… through the call center of the service provider.

155
00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:41.179
Glyn Povah: to the agent on their screen, so they can identify the customer in that way. Unfortunately, it's quite easy to spoof those numbers these days, so there's no guarantee that the number presented to the agent or into the relying party will be the number that's calling, into that call center.

156
00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:54.570
Glyn Povah: Of course, you know, that's used alongside, normally, knowledge-based authentication, but I think it's pretty easy… you know, I'm pretty sure that my name, address, date of birth, my first pet's name is widely available on the dark web now.

157
00:18:54.880 --> 00:19:01.399
Glyn Povah: certainly based on the number of phishing attacks I get, most of which I don't respond to.

158
00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:06.880
Glyn Povah: then, you know, that data, I assume, is widely available, and therefore, it's pretty easy for

159
00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:19.270
Glyn Povah: for scammers to get past the security in the banks, or any other relying party where there's value, if you want to get into British Airways and, you know, access my Avios balance, or whatever else.

160
00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:29.180
Glyn Povah: There's plenty of, I think, relying parties that are much broader than finance. Healthcare, I think, is another one where it's very sensitive in terms of the data protection and the data that's stored.

161
00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:39.779
Glyn Povah: by healthcare providers. I think there's many different verticals where, you know, call center authentication is vital that it's done in a very secure way. And actually, more than that.

162
00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:44.310
Glyn Povah: And we'll come onto this in a privacy-preserving way for the subscribers.

163
00:19:46.210 --> 00:20:01.189
Glyn Povah: So, just to sum up what we were trying to do with our solution, and obviously you'll get a demo in a minute, so you'll be able to kind of bring it to life a bit, and then maybe ask any questions, but we're trying to achieve fast authentication, so to reduce the time it takes to authenticate callers.

164
00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:10.669
Glyn Povah: And a lot of that is about, you know, eliminating or at least, reducing the amount of knowledge-based authentication or one-time passcodes that are sent to customers.

165
00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:18.619
Glyn Povah: Much stronger security, so mitigating the known risks around SIM swap fraud, phishing, CLI spoofing, and stolen data.

166
00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:26.230
Glyn Povah: And, as I mentioned, enhanced privacy. So, actually, leveraging some of the benefits of decentralized identity and verifiable credentials.

167
00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:31.530
Glyn Povah: By allowing customers to share information with brands and relying parties.

168
00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:43.000
Glyn Povah: But without them having to actually share the actual data. So, things like selective disclosure, which are really, I think, great features of the VC technology. We really wanted to demonstrate that as well.

169
00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:54.109
Nick: Thank you, Glenn. And just kind of jumping in, you'll notice the change in color of the slide. So, GSMA, obviously, as Glenn mentioned, we're part of this project, so…

170
00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:10.139
Nick: These are not my slides, but they're certainly part of the story and the context for why this solution and why we think it's important. In terms of just volumes of scam threat, what they're seeing across the globe is $1 trillion worth of scams in 2024.

171
00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:21.850
Nick: And circa 25% of unknown calls are scams or fraud, and looking at my own phone, it seems like 75% are scams or fraud. It seems to be a kind of growing problem.

172
00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:29.810
Nick: So it's definitely a situation that's already quite bad, and… and not getting better anytime soon.

173
00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:36.180
Nick: There's also a kind of hidden cost behind this, an emotional cost, I guess.

174
00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:48.010
Nick: the victims would feel, and so you can see some of the stats there, but, you know, even people getting depressed or losing sleep, I guess, if the results are particularly bad. So there's an economic impact.

175
00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:52.969
Nick: For, kind of, fraud, obviously, and then there's, like, an emotional one as well, which we shouldn't discount.

176
00:21:53.370 --> 00:22:10.279
Nick: And then also, as GSA may point out with this slide, there's actually the people that are, kind of forced to work in these scam centres, so there's a kind of, probably a hidden set of victims in here that are actually helping, people carry out these scams and these fraud attacks, so…

177
00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:22.010
Nick: There's a lot of different perspectives here, and the last few were brought to us by GSMA. So what we'll do now is we'll run through the demo. Glenn and I, we're going to risk,

178
00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:36.840
Nick: a live demo, but we kind of bottled out at the last minute, just trying to get things configured, so what we're showing is a kind of video. The demo is quite short, so it'll just take about a minute and a half, and there's some other kind of music besides, so I'll let that run just now.

179
00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:17.619
