00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:33.890
Audio shared by Nick: Please open the notification and confirm your identity by clicking the confirm button.

182
00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:51.059
Audio shared by Nick: Thank you. Your identity has been verified successfully. Please hold while we connect you to one of our healthcare advisors.

183
00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:02.579
Nick: So that's it. It's very, very quick, and…

184
00:24:02.900 --> 00:24:13.439
Nick: I guess it's kind of, like, payment for, or for not being able to, actually, run the demo live. If anyone who's based in the UK or Spain

185
00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:17.639
Nick: is wanting to actually participate in the POC,

186
00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:32.439
Nick: Maybe you can get your email address and things to Eric, and then we'll be able to kind of set you up inside the POC if you want to try it out for yourself. just… and those specific countries, just because of the carrier coverage that we have.

187
00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:49.529
Nick: But I… and we'll talk a little bit more about what was the technology that… that was in part of that… the POC. And really what we're doing there is, when a user calls a call center, they would have previously gone through a kind of onboarding flow.

188
00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:55.809
Nick: With, let's call it Telefonica, which… O2, sorry, which is one of Telefonica's brands in the UK.

189
00:24:55.860 --> 00:25:03.050
Nick: And during that onboarding flow, they can be set up and have their account mapped to a wallet address.

190
00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:17.930
Nick: And that can be a wallet address that… in the POC, it was a separate application, but probably when we expect to see this at production scale, likely this will be an SDK that's embedded inside the carrier's own app.

191
00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:29.640
Nick: So this will hopefully be an app that they would have already, and then an address associated with their account. And so, when they set that up, they are sent,

192
00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:45.839
Nick: an O2 customer credential, and when they then phone up the call center, that one of two things can happen. So, in the example that we showed in the POC, and the way the POC actually runs, if you try it for yourself.

193
00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:51.769
Nick: is that we send a DITCOM message, a push notification to

194
00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:58.619
Nick: The caller's wallet address, and they are then notified about that, and it literally says, as you can see.

195
00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:15.080
Nick: Are you calling the call center? And they reply, and obviously send a DITCOM message back, confirming that that is their account. We also, as part of the POC, just to prove that we could do it as well, because it opens up a lot more interesting use cases

196
00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:26.309
Nick: is we actually also put a credential inside that wallet as well. We used the DITCOM message just to show that we could do that, but also it was literally one less click.

197
00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:38.399
Nick: than using a credential presentation, so we went with the kind of simplest and cleanest UX that we could to show how easily this could be implemented.

198
00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:57.139
Nick: But actually, you could also just do this through a credential as well. But what we like about this, and the reason I kind of tend to call it a kind of Trojan horse, is certainly for the last 6 years or so through Doc Labs, we've spoken to a lot of different companies, lots of different use cases.

199
00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:58.900
Nick: About how we…

200
00:26:59.110 --> 00:27:18.530
Nick: put credentials and wallets and that type of technology inside of either their organization or out to their customers, who could be end users. And often the challenge there is how hard it is to make people aware of this technology, how hard it is to educate people, and often

201
00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:31.130
Nick: that can be, at least I've found, the factor that can scare people away, and they'll say, well, I'll do it after someone else is due, or I'll come back next year when, you know, EIDAS2 is in, and people are starting to use wallets more readily.

202
00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:49.719
Nick: using this type of technology. But actually, I like this use case so much because it actually gets, users using wallets in an extremely simple use case. And if we think about, instead of a did call message, actually a credential request being sent to the user's phone.

203
00:27:49.830 --> 00:28:06.050
Nick: and the user clicking, yes, I'm speaking to you and sending a cryptographically signed presentation back to the call center operator, you've actually started to get potentially at significant scale credentials and wallets, and users actually using them.

204
00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:08.889
Nick: In, kind of, production environments.

205
00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:22.010
Nick: And as I was saying there, like, the O2 wallet penetration, I think, Glenn, if I'm not giving the game away here, Glenn, but I think we're kind of wondering, like, what's the distribution like of wallets, or applications in this case?

206
00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:28.709
Nick: And I think Glenn was kind of of the view about maybe three-quarters that, you know, 70% or 75% of monthly

207
00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:47.490
Nick: subscribers were actually regularly using the mobile app, and so the distribution there was actually pretty strong, and so you can actually have pretty big scale. I think O2's customer base in the UK for that type of subscriber is about 15 million, so you can start to get a sense of what that is. That's one carrier.

208
00:28:47.490 --> 00:29:00.009
Nick: and that's the lead carrier inside that country, within the UK, they would obviously have, you know, provide similar services to virtual carriers as well, and so that base could be much, much bigger.

209
00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:13.619
Nick: And that's why I think this use case is particularly exciting beyond a telecoms person, which I'm not. Like, I'm more interested, or my background is more in decentralized identity, and that's why I have found this particularly, kind of, interesting as well.

210
00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:27.229
Nick: But it's not all, you know, it's… it's life as a box of chocolates, as they say, and it's… you know, there's still some challenges to overcome with this. You know, like, we… Glenn and I talk about, like, do carriers want to be…

211
00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:37.610
Nick: you know, verifiable credential issuers. You know, they're concerned about how that would work, or educating users, or any liabilities that that might involve.

212
00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:48.620
Nick: You know, we need to make sure that the wallet address is assigned to a user, as the onboard, which is a little bit more simple, but what would you do with existing?

213
00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:51.359
Nick: Customers, how do you manage that?

214
00:29:51.640 --> 00:30:04.960
Nick: process, so there's still some things to overcome, and there's more besides that, but I just think it's a really, really useful use case and a potential for mass adoption if you're coming from the kind of verifiable credential side.

215
00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:22.110
Nick: And then it's what this builds into, like, what can we do now? And that's why we put the credential in there, that's why we didn't just go with the DIDCOM message, is that it opens up lots more use cases. I'm not touching all of these, but I mean, you can get to the point where

216
00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:29.390
Nick: Carriers can start to become potential identities as service providers, if they have a high degree of confidence in the data that they have.

217
00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:45.679
Nick: They have definitely unique data that other organizations don't have. They probably know your address in most cases. They probably know how well you pay your bills, or they should do. They know your mobile number, which is quite powerful in and of itself.

218
00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:56.789
Nick: And so you can start to layer other information on top of that, and potentially, with the user's consent, start to, sell that data to… into other use cases.

219
00:30:57.090 --> 00:31:09.249
Nick: You can also get partner onboarding and things like that as well, so if, you know, Telefonica or O2 were to do a partnership with someone like Spotify, maybe they can have something where there's 20% off

220
00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:22.310
Nick: Spotify subscriptions for O2 customers. That type of stuff obviously happens today, but there's quite a high instance of fraud. Again, people claiming to be O2 customers that aren't, and how does Spotify verify that?

221
00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:25.790
Nick: Well, actually, here would be a nice use case of using credentials to do that.

222
00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:29.129
Nick: And you could, you know, pass that amongst your partners.

223
00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:47.929
Nick: Even things like delegated authority, which is becoming more and more. Everyone's talking about agents these days, and so you could even start to have a customer credential as one evidence of who someone is, and potentially start to delegate some of that authority to an agent down the road as well.

224
00:31:47.930 --> 00:31:55.599
Nick: So there's… there's lots of opportunities that exist now that we have some of these core, kind of tools in place.

225
00:31:56.450 --> 00:31:59.099
Nick: And just because this is a…

226
00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:11.559
Nick: a group, I imagine, that is kind of well-versed in, kind of, standards and things. I thought it would be important just to quickly highlight, at least from the Truvera, the DocLab stack that was used during this demo.

227
00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:19.750
Nick: we're, using, kind of, W3C, credentials here, JSON-LD,

228
00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:29.600
Nick: We're using a couple of different DID methods. We use the checked blockchain, which is a third-party blockchain to us, and we also can use DID key as well.

229
00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:45.470
Nick: And then we also, as I said, the… the transfer protocol we use was Dipcom for the messaging, and then we also use, OpenID Connect, for, assurance and kind of presentation of that credential, were that to be used.

230
00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:50.939
Nick: So hopefully that kind of gives us some insight into the kind of toolset that we used there.

231
00:32:52.070 --> 00:33:02.740
