284
00:41:05.530 --> 00:41:09.820
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Yeah, sure you are. I'm gonna hang up and call, and then we'll try it again.

285
00:41:09.820 --> 00:41:17.839
Glyn Povah: Yeah, sounds very wise, Kylie. I would do the same, I think. But I think, you know, the general point from the telecoms perspective is

286
00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:22.730
Glyn Povah: You know, we want to uplift our capabilities such that…

287
00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:35.579
Glyn Povah: you can prove that you are who you say you are, whether you're a consumer, or a brand, or a relying party, so two-way communications in a cryptographically secure way, in a deterministic way, and that is not possible today, so…

288
00:41:35.580 --> 00:41:35.980
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Now.

289
00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:42.950
Glyn Povah: you know, this project and a number of other projects, another one which is called Open Verifiable Communications, where Eric's involved as well.

290
00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:48.039
Glyn Povah: It's all about, building that cryptographically secure capability.

291
00:41:48.540 --> 00:42:02.929
Glyn Povah: from relying parties and call centres calling consumers, and this project is obviously identifying and providing capability the other way around. So I think, overall, what we want to get to is cryptographically secure voice and messaging two-way, on all carrier networks. Now.

292
00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:20.140
Glyn Povah: you know, it's easy for me to say that, it's not a small thing. We're doing a lot of probabilistic solutions today, which have, you know, have actually been quite impactful in the market in reducing scams and fraud, but I think, for me, it's on the journey towards cryptographically secure, and…

293
00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:23.149
Glyn Povah: That's the… that's the final destination.

294
00:42:23.450 --> 00:42:30.229
Glyn Povah: Which, you know, will take some time to reach, but it's certainly the strategy of the industry to get there.

295
00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:47.689
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Real quick clarification question, because when you say call center, a lot of times we don't realize we're talking to a call center, right? So if I call my bank, is that… I mean, that's the bank… to me, I'm calling my bank, but that's a call center that's behind there that's… that's managing all that, so…

296
00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:52.919
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): I think, for us who are not part of the telco system, we think, well, how often do I call a call center?

297
00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:54.060
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): And…

298
00:42:54.060 --> 00:43:02.980
Glyn Povah: Yeah, so if you've got a phone, yeah, any brand you're calling, whether it's your bank, whether it's, the HMRC for taxes,

299
00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:17.640
Glyn Povah: or whether it's, yeah, your healthcare provider, yeah, this is a solution that will work for all of those, and it will be… the idea is we implement it in all call center-as-a-service technology solutions. In fact, for the POC, we integrated with Amazon Connect, which is one of the leading ones.

300
00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:19.269
Glyn Povah: There are many out there.

301
00:43:19.720 --> 00:43:20.630
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Right, okay.

302
00:43:22.010 --> 00:43:28.319
Nick: All right, Glenn, will I pass back to you to, get into, what actually happened in the POC, and what's our…

303
00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:30.360
Nick: What's our experience been so far?

304
00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:31.690
Glyn Povah: Thanks, Nick.

305
00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:41.759
Glyn Povah: Yeah, so short summary of where we got to with the POC. I think, you know, we did prove that the use case was really well understood, and I think made sense to most people.

306
00:43:42.070 --> 00:43:49.239
Glyn Povah: A lot of us call brands every day, and we have to go through this frustrating process of proving that we are who we say we are with

307
00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:54.819
Glyn Povah: Mostly knowledge-based information, which takes a long time, and it's not always successful.

308
00:43:55.270 --> 00:44:06.259
Glyn Povah: Takes up to 5 minutes in some cases, so we've proven that actually we can reduce that authentication time period and do it in a more secure way to less than 60 seconds.

309
00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:10.870
Glyn Povah: And we've done that in over 500 calls,

310
00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:25.290
Glyn Povah: with 70 demos given at the Mobile Congress when we were showing it, on the demo day in Barcelona. So I think it has generated huge interest, amongst relying parties and call centers and service providers, as well as

311
00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:33.299
Glyn Povah: you know, people in the wider industry. So, it's been, I think, successful for what we wanted to achieve for the first stage, and now we need to

312
00:44:33.620 --> 00:44:37.160
Glyn Povah: kind of take it to the next stage, you know…

313
00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:45.710
Glyn Povah: And that is really looking at it from, I guess, three angles. And the first one is the commercial perspective. I think, you know, with all of these things.

314
00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:53.219
Glyn Povah: We can… we can demonstrate technology and get good feedback for it, and… and even get consumers engaged in it.

315
00:44:53.320 --> 00:45:02.350
Glyn Povah: But actually, you know, there needs to be a business case for people in the ecosystem, the partners in the ecosystem, to…

316
00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:10.139
Glyn Povah: To participate and build this as a long-standing ecosystem where everyone can actually extract some value.

317
00:45:10.420 --> 00:45:12.660
Glyn Povah: Now, I guess from the carrier perspective.

318
00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:18.529
Glyn Povah: And I think this is a general point, actually, not just for carriers, it's a general point for…

319
00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:27.690
Glyn Povah: For wallets and credentials is there has to be some sort of incentive for issuers to issue, and those incentives do not exist today.

320
00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:40.479
Glyn Povah: And that is why, predominantly, in my opinion, we only see government wallets and government initiatives offering government services. What has been lacking in the European identity wallets, but also in many other

321
00:45:40.610 --> 00:45:42.210
Glyn Povah: Countries and regions.

322
00:45:42.360 --> 00:46:01.040
Glyn Povah: there's a distinct lack of engagement with private sector and commercial players, who I think would be… many would be very willing to issue credentials, telcos included, but, you know, there's a cost to doing so, and it's not a core part of our business today.

323
00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:08.159
Glyn Povah: So, we were looking to find a business case for us to, you know, cover the costs of deploying this technology.

324
00:46:08.630 --> 00:46:17.830
Glyn Povah: such that, you know, it made an ecosystem work for us in the long term. So, you know, I guess one of the next steps we're doing in the project is we're looking at the cost model.

325
00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:30.200
Glyn Povah: And some potential revenue models of how we can, you know, extract some value. Obviously, we want value for our subscribers as well, and we want value for the relying parties and technology providers like, like, you know, Doc Labs.

326
00:46:31.740 --> 00:46:36.830
Glyn Povah: From the tech perspective, I actually think, you know, in some ways, this is, quite a challenging one.

327
00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:49.490
Glyn Povah: We've obviously demonstrated the tech, and we spun up this POC very quickly, actually, including the call center part, including the doc labs part, and the wallet, and the credential part, very simply, and the integration with the carrier network.

328
00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:51.470
Glyn Povah: But actually.

329
00:46:51.800 --> 00:47:00.920
Glyn Povah: I think, you know, it's not quite clear what's happening in the evolution of wallets and credentials, other than the government initiatives, which are very well known.

330
00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:08.329
Glyn Povah: I think, you know, like I said, the private sector has had really no, input into this. Apple is playing with this a little bit in the US.

331
00:47:08.740 --> 00:47:16.839
Glyn Povah: in, you know, allowing consumers to put identity credentials in their OEM wallets, and others are playing around with their stuff, but…

332
00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:26.399
Glyn Povah: I guess we're thinking about what is the… what is the end state for this kind of technology? You know, is it going to be about wallets,

333
00:47:26.930 --> 00:47:35.509
Glyn Povah: that are, you know, downloaded by consumers? Probably not. Consumers don't really have much interest in downloading things, because it gets in the way of them doing stuff.

334
00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:39.610
Glyn Povah: Is it about native wallets? You know, so I guess we're talking about, you know.

335
00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:46.829
Glyn Povah: predominantly Apple and Google, but other device vendors who are building wallets natively in their operating systems.

336
00:47:47.150 --> 00:47:55.100
Glyn Povah: you know, that works really well, consumer perspective. You know, we're obviously all very used to that, from having, you know, boarding passes and…

337
00:47:55.610 --> 00:48:04.950
Glyn Povah: Loyalty cards, hotel keys, even, in our native wallets, and that stuff works really well, because you know where it is, and you know where to get it, and you know where it's going to be on your phone.

338
00:48:05.450 --> 00:48:11.349
Glyn Povah: You know, so you could say, you know, to Nick's point earlier, why not put carrier phone number credential in the native wallet?

339
00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:27.959
Glyn Povah: I think, you know, we need to see… we need to see where that might take us. Government wallets, you know, we could… we could put… we could put, our credentials in a government wallet. It doesn't seem there's much interest from government to do that at the moment. They seem to be trying to keep the private sector out, which probably is a little short-sighted.

340
00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:37.210
Glyn Povah: And then we've got alternative solutions, which we could deploy, things like our number-verify SIM-based, APIs, which would, you know, probably take away from…

341
00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:50.129
Glyn Povah: some of the… or many of the benefits of deploying VC-based technology, where you've got things like, you know, selective disclosure and all the other benefits that come from involving the customer in the journey, albeit with a little bit of friction. So,

342
00:48:50.440 --> 00:49:02.150
Glyn Povah: So yeah, a big focus for the next step for the project will be analyzing different technical solutions, with a particular focus, I guess, on the wallets and where the credentials go, and how that works with other ecosystem players.

343
00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:05.520
Glyn Povah: And I guess finally,

344
00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:10.050
Glyn Povah: Now, we'll need to do a lot of work on the privacy, security, and trust perspective.

345
00:49:10.330 --> 00:49:18.050
Glyn Povah: I think I talked at the beginning, and Nick mentioned the scams that are happening, and the trust that's declining in the carrier networks and voice and messaging, so…

346
00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:22.559
Glyn Povah: You know, it's compelling for us to invest in these technologies,

347
00:49:23.590 --> 00:49:35.929
Glyn Povah: But, you know, not at any cost, obviously. I think we've seen with Stir Shaken in the US, that, you know, the cost to the carriers were very, very high, and the benefits maybe haven't flowed through in the way that was expected, so we want to avoid that situation again, certainly in Europe.

348
00:49:36.830 --> 00:49:42.650
Glyn Povah: And, you know, we need to see that whether, you know, consumers really want to…

349
00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:49.290
Glyn Povah: adopt this technology, and maybe the government wallets will help with that from a kind of halo perspective, an education perspective. I think they probably will.

350
00:49:49.460 --> 00:49:58.710
Glyn Povah: To get… to get consumers more used to using credentials on their phones, maybe in… maybe in native apps, maybe not even in, you know, dedicated wallets.

351
00:49:59.020 --> 00:50:01.159
Glyn Povah: So I think that's going to help us quite a lot.

352
00:50:01.360 --> 00:50:10.939
Glyn Povah: But I would say, you know, do consumers really care about, their security and privacy? Probably not very much. Probably more than they used to.

353
00:50:11.060 --> 00:50:14.159
Glyn Povah: Given all the scams, and frauds that are happening.

354
00:50:14.310 --> 00:50:23.140
Glyn Povah: I think if you talk to a lot of… a lot of our customers, they'll probably assume that if something does go wrong, someone's gonna pick up the tab.

355
00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:28.409
Glyn Povah: Either the bank, or the telco, or maybe even the relying party, or the… or the,

356
00:50:28.830 --> 00:50:32.580
Glyn Povah: The… the networks in terms of the credit and debit cards, so…

357
00:50:32.870 --> 00:50:41.590
Glyn Povah: I think there's a lot of complacency by customers, they just want to get on and get stuff done, buy those… buy those tickets, you know, go on… go on that trip.

358
00:50:41.870 --> 00:50:50.469
Glyn Povah: And actually, stuff that gets in their way, I think they find annoying and frustrating. So, I know all of us, probably on this call, you know, are very…

359
00:50:50.970 --> 00:51:01.140
Glyn Povah: focused on privacy and security. I wouldn't say that's so well, you know, understood by… by… by most consumers.

360
00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:19.370
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): But I'm… so, I mean, are you guys… sometimes it thinks that there's all this work and infrastructure and years of development, and it just gets… it gets, boiled down to, like, a feature.

361
00:51:19.490 --> 00:51:25.370
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): You know, like, if you've got the Telefonica app.

362
00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:44.879
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): And then, you know, all of a sudden, you've got a new feature that appears that… and we want to put it, you know, front and center, look, there's a great thing with all these capabilities, where all the capabilities, like we… you guys mentioned, it… the technology is hidden, it's behind the scenes, and what's important is the value that it adds to the interaction, or whatever.

363
00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:49.959
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): And it just shows up as a tiny little icon or feature that, you know, most of us don't know about.

364
00:51:50.020 --> 00:51:57.750
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): And so, have you explored looking at the, you know, having some of the, the, the app

365
00:51:58.420 --> 00:52:01.709
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): costs the Telefonica apps, I,

366
00:52:01.740 --> 00:52:17.800
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): take on some of these costs of development, or whatever. Or, you know, you're replacing some of the things that exist now, and there might be a transition period, but… but you're going to be eventually doing things in a different way, and so those costs

367
00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:26.930
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): I mean, I think this is the hardest part for, you know, for you guys. You've shown that it has value, economic value, but still, you need to get it into the…

368
00:52:27.160 --> 00:52:28.770
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): You know, into the ecosystem.

369
00:52:28.960 --> 00:52:38.110
Glyn Povah: Yeah, we need to… we need to get it into the ecosystem, into our apps. I think, you know, it reminds me of a famous Steve Jobs quote, which was, technology should be beautiful or it should be invisible.

370
00:52:38.300 --> 00:52:42.110
Glyn Povah: I think, you know, we're probably moving more towards the invisible here,

371
00:52:42.860 --> 00:52:57.969
Glyn Povah: At least as far as, you know, the distribution into the carrier apps and integration into the carrier apps, and I like your idea of, you know, just that new feature that suddenly just appears, in the app, Eric, so I think that that is somewhere we want to go, but we need to be mindful of the cost, you know.

372
00:52:58.670 --> 00:53:11.380
Glyn Povah: A lot of the costs are internal costs today, so we do a lot of our own software development. Obviously, a lot of the carrier apps are about checking your bill, and maybe getting in contact with customer service, with any queries. Sometimes there's some…

373
00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:19.359
Glyn Povah: kind of customer offers in there as well that takes you to those. But I think it's great to provide more utility in the app.

374
00:53:19.910 --> 00:53:24.649
Glyn Povah: So, having that new feature, I think, could be quite powerful from that perspective, but, you know.

375
00:53:24.920 --> 00:53:35.869
Glyn Povah: We do… we do… we do want to do so in a cost-effective way as well, so we can't… we can't be, I guess, you know, having… having massive increase in costs for the sake of adding that… that new feature.

376
00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:55.529
Nick: I think as well, like, part of, like, you know, Glenn and I kind of working together on this, obviously, Glenn's got very much a carrier perspective, but we also look at, you know, you know, contact center as a service to CCAS providers as well. They would also be, kind of, they're also interested in this technology, and they will have different priorities, obviously.

377
00:53:55.770 --> 00:54:10.500
Nick: all of the above our businesses, and they'll be looking for, kind of revenue, cost-saving opportunities, but I think each organization and sector will have a different perspective about how you can generate that revenue, or how you generate that cost-saving, so…

378
00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:26.549
Nick: I think there's kind of multiple, kind of angles here for us to… to try and see if we can… we can make the business case work, because clearly, as is often the case, the technology implementation, whilst not trivial, can often be something that gets solved quite early on.

379
00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:39.569
Nick: And it's getting the business and the legal and the compliance stuff that follows lined up so it can actually be rolled out at scale. So I think those are the challenges that we certainly still face. As an interesting point, I see the question in from Neil.

380
00:54:39.750 --> 00:54:53.610
Nick: Which I think kind of links back to, you know, I think this is a two-way street, and so you have the notion that, you know, the call centers want to know who's calling into them, and that's absolutely the way it should be, and we should make that easier for the users, but of course.

381
00:54:53.610 --> 00:55:13.010
Nick: like, we're all consumers as well, and I'm sure we got all, you know, way too many calls that sometimes our phone flags up as suspect spam, and we also really want to know who's calling us, which is the other part of the coin, really, and I know, Glenn, you're working, as some of the other people in this group are on, I think it's OVM, is it? Which is another.

382
00:55:13.010 --> 00:55:14.660
Glyn Povah: OVC, OVC.

383
00:55:14.660 --> 00:55:20.780
Nick: OVC, apologies, where it's trying to identify the company calling, which I think kneels to your point.

384
00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:39.489
Glyn Povah: Yeah, and in fact, you know, we're already… we've already started that journey in our messaging service, which is called RCS, or you might have heard RBM, which is the enterprise messaging capability that we've now launched, which is an evolution of SMS, so it's… it's kind of like a WhatsApp experience on your phone, but native from your carrier.

385
00:55:39.790 --> 00:55:48.260
Glyn Povah: It's available on all iPhone and Android devices from most carriers globally now, and that… that has one new feature, which is called Verified Sender.

386
00:55:48.610 --> 00:55:57.370
Glyn Povah: And Verify Sender allows you to check who the sender is, and it's got a little green tick, and it'll tell you that, the carrier has

387
00:55:57.500 --> 00:56:07.870
Glyn Povah: verified and vetted the sender of that message before it arrives on your device. So I think we are starting that journey, and we've got production deployments of that with many big brands in many of our markets.

388
00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:13.980
Glyn Povah: So yes, we are very much incentivized, I think, and, you know, motivated

389
00:56:14.470 --> 00:56:24.970
Glyn Povah: To move, to a situation where we've got that capability to do verified voice and messaging from brands to consumers, and vice versa, and ideally in a cryptographically secure way.

390
00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:35.780
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Excellent. Well, we've got a couple… a couple more minutes, so if… we probably have time for one last question, if anyone has.

391
00:56:41.810 --> 00:56:43.780
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): no governance questions from Scott.

392
00:56:47.710 --> 00:56:57.030
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Maybe, just a statement that hopefully these technologies will turn, phones in from hated devices to useful technologies again.

393
00:56:57.030 --> 00:56:59.550
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): You hate your phone? People love their phones.

394
00:56:59.550 --> 00:57:02.809
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): I love my phone, except for phone calls.

395
00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:15.380
Glyn Povah: I mean, Carly, it's very much our mission to bring trust back to communications. I mean, this is one of the proof points and evidence points, and, you know, there's a whole bunch of work going on in the industry, in the telcos, in GSMA, behind that.

396
00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:21.510
Glyn Povah: projects, I guess. That is our mission, absolutely. It won't happen overnight, but we're going to get there, I do believe.

397
00:57:22.120 --> 00:57:22.690
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Fantastic.

398
00:57:23.810 --> 00:57:34.220
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): I can make a couple comments. I can make just a comment with the one minute we have left. So, you know, based in Seattle, I do have some contacts in with…

399
00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:52.700
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): with T-Mobile, and I know that they're doing a lot, really, to address security. You know, a close friend was in the security task force there. You know, the challenge exists not necessarily bringing the technology, it's getting people to agree to the governance.

400
00:57:52.870 --> 00:58:11.919
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): to come and sit down and figure out how we're going to address this collaboratively. And that, you know, in a variety of different arenas that we're dealing with, that is going to be the sticky point. I think that technology is now, you know, reaching achievability.

401
00:58:11.920 --> 00:58:14.069
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): The question is, is…

402
00:58:14.110 --> 00:58:26.150
Scott Perry (Digital Governance Institute): Now is, you know, relying parties and governing authorities going to, you know, take the chance and start putting in governance systems to manage these things?

403
00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:36.829
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Compliance, yeah. I think a lot of what's pushing this is the regulatory obligations, right? That are saying, guys, fix this, do something.

404
00:58:36.940 --> 00:58:40.679
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): So, I think that's… that's spurring a lot of this development on.

405
00:58:42.370 --> 00:58:48.929
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): But we've… so we're out of time, so I think we'll just close it, and thank you for all the comments. Thank you for the presentation, Nick and Glenn.

